  body: "# Imprisonment disqualification and payability\n\n## Authored interpretation\n\nThe captured
    general guidance distinguishes disqualification from payment during imprisonment from entitlement
    itself, and describes resumption when the relevant conditions remain satisfied unless entitlement
    has terminated.\n\n## Scope and exceptions\n\nThis is not a rule for every benefit or detention. DMG
    12015 routes JSA, IS, Pension Credit and ESA to benefit-specific chapters; DMG 12016 routes UC, PIP
    and New Style JSA/ESA to ADM. The listed exceptions, types of custody, mental-health law, overseas
    circumstances and termination decisions remain separate questions.\n\n## Inspected source passages\n\
    \n- [DMG 12003, PDF page 3](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/651bcf2e6dfda6000d8e39d2/dmgch12.pdf#page=3)
    — DMG Vol 3 Ch 12: Imprisonment.\n- [DMG 12015, PDF page 3](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/651bcf2e6dfda6000d8e39d2/dmgch12.pdf#page=3)
    — DMG Vol 3 Ch 12: Imprisonment.\n- [DMG 12016, PDF page 3](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/651bcf2e6dfda6000d8e39d2/dmgch12.pdf#page=3)
    — DMG Vol 3 Ch 12: Imprisonment.\n\n## Evidence and review status\n\nModel-assisted interpretation
    of selected passages in the 15 September 2026 frozen source snapshot. Specialist review is required.
    Source acquisition, source revision, legal effective dates and this record’s authoring time are separate.\n\
    \nThis is an independent experimental publication, not an official DWP document, individual advice,
    an entitlement decision or an executable rule. Machine-extracted text may alter reading order, footnotes
    and characters. No PDF visual inspection, whole-chapter coverage or behavioural evaluation pass is
    claimed by this record.\n\n- `dmg-vol3-ch12`: PDF SHA-256 `be79f9d6f8baf23290b8d6af5f384d31c2435b960fe3a59c14febd510fbaa9fe`;
    extracted pages SHA-256 `989a5b292ba1f6129362ddc7ee736133798156d58c3539be2d0232fad5c92660`.\n"
  semantic_relations:
  - target: term/common-criminal-custody
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The source limits the relevant general detention to criminal proceedings or
      imprisonment by a criminal court and expressly distinguishes civil proceedings.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol3-ch12/0008
      quote: "12027 The term “imprisonment or detention in legal custody” means\n\n\n1. any detention
        connected with criminal proceedings1or\n\n\n2. imprisonment imposed by a criminal court2.\n\n\n\
        A claimant sentenced to imprisonment as a result of civil proceedings is not disqualified 2.\n\
        \n\n                                                                                       1 R(P)
        2/57; 2 R(S) 8/79"
      locator: DMG 12027
  - target: term/common-sp-remand-outcome
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The State Pension remand passage has its own pending-outcome suspension and
      subsequent-sentence qualifications; the general payability distinction does not settle that
      case.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol3-ch12/0005
      quote: "12020 A person who is remanded in custody is not a prisoner1 in accordance with DMG 12018
        so can be\npaid SP unless\n\n\n1. a sentence of imprisonment or detention in legal custody as
        a result of criminal proceedings 2or\n\n\n2. a sentence of detention where paragraph 12018 3.1
        or 4. applies3or\n\n\n3. a suspended sentence of imprisonment4\n\n\nis later imposed on the person
        for the offence5. Therefore DMs should suspend SP when a person is\nremanded in custody pending
        the outcome of proceedings.\n\n\n                   1 SP Regs, reg 3(1); 2 reg 3(2)(a); 3 reg
        3(2)(b); 4 reg 3(2)(c); Criminal Justice Act 03, s\n                   189;\n                \
        \   5 SP Regs, reg 3(1)"
      locator: DMG 12020
    - page: page/dmg-vol3-ch12/0003
      quote: "12003 Claimants are disqualified from receiving these benefits when they are imprisoned
        or detained in\n\nlegal custody following criminal proceedings 1. Disqualification only affects
        the payability of the benefit\n\nnot claimants’ entitlement to that benefit2. This means that
        when the conditions supporting the\ndisqualification no longer apply, payment of benefit can resume
        providing all the conditions of\nentitlement remain satisfied. Unless entitlement has been terminated
        during the period of imprisonment\nor detention in legal custody, a claimant should not be required
        to make a new claim to benefit on\nrelease.\n\n\n                                            \
        \                   1 SS CB Act 92, s 113(1)(b); 2 CDLA/1930/04\n\n\n12004 - 12014\n\n\n\nEffect
        of imprisonment on JSA, IS, SPC and ESA"
      locator: DMG 12003
